WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.460
*映画 The theatrical

00:20.060 --> 00:23.020
This series of puzzle-solving games

00:23.230 --> 00:27.730
has been a worldwide success with more than 7,300,000 fans.

00:32.280 --> 00:35.860
Professor Layton now stars in more eagerly awaited sequels.

00:36.820 --> 00:40.120
This film follows from the fourth installment of the series

00:40.330 --> 00:44.000
where Layton and Luke meet for their first adventure.

00:45.420 --> 00:49.290
And now their story will play out on the movie screen.

00:54.430 --> 00:59.370
Luke, I've finally solved this complicated case.

00:59.370 --> 01:02.540
What, you've solved the mystery?

01:02.540 --> 01:04.370
Professor, don't shine that in my face.

01:04.370 --> 01:07.990
The suspect escaped from here.

01:07.990 --> 01:11.880
But I don't see an exit here.

01:13.110 --> 01:17.940
This all began when something that should have been impossible to steal <i>was</i> stolen.

01:18.580 --> 01:22.030
After the threat arrived, the bell stopped ringing,

01:22.030 --> 01:24.240
but the bell itself was not stolen.

01:25.030 --> 01:27.270
The biggest clue in solving this case...

01:29.120 --> 01:31.860
lies in the message at the entrance of the tower.

01:31.860 --> 01:36.690
"When the objects in the sky gather together, the path to the future will be carved out."

01:36.690 --> 01:38.490
So what does that mean?

01:42.670 --> 01:45.200
Be patient, Luke.

01:45.200 --> 01:46.920
You're an English gentleman, remember?

01:48.000 --> 01:50.080
These objects portray the sky,

01:50.080 --> 01:53.510
they cooperate with one another.

01:54.100 --> 01:57.090
Professor, are you sure that'll work?

01:57.090 --> 02:00.040
Ah, just watch.

02:24.720 --> 02:25.900
This is...

02:39.170 --> 02:41.390
To our dear viewers,

02:41.390 --> 02:44.750
this is how Professor Layton unravelled the mystery,

02:44.750 --> 02:49.240
and solved another incident that was troubling London.

02:49.240 --> 02:53.400
There's only one person who could've stopped that bell.

02:54.000 --> 02:55.070
And that is you!

02:59.140 --> 03:01.570
I'll remember this, Layton!

03:04.360 --> 03:05.080
Don Paolo!

03:16.590 --> 03:17.540
Luke!

03:17.540 --> 03:17.950
Yes!

03:17.950 --> 03:19.880
Flora, wait here.

03:20.340 --> 03:21.090
Professor!

03:21.090 --> 03:22.590
After him, Barton!

03:22.590 --> 03:23.700
Y-Yes, sir!

03:28.210 --> 03:33.990
And thus, as usual, he made the headlines of the London Times.

03:34.930 --> 03:38.190
Not only is he an excellent archaeologist,

03:38.190 --> 03:42.700
but he's also a renowned mystery lover who solves many puzzles around the world.

03:43.080 --> 03:48.140
In other words, he's the biggest mystery lover among London's gentlemen.

03:49.500 --> 03:52.320
I'm his assistant, Luke.

03:52.320 --> 03:57.000
Everyday I sort his enigmatic mail and requests for solving puzzles,

03:57.000 --> 03:58.580
serve him his favourite tea,

03:59.370 --> 04:03.180
and help him tidy his things, as he isn't really good at that.

04:04.810 --> 04:06.720
As for me...

04:06.720 --> 04:10.060
I guess I'm good at understanding what animals say.

04:10.930 --> 04:15.270
I've forgotten how many years it's been since I joined him as an apprentice.

04:15.270 --> 04:17.940
As we embark on our journeys to solve mysteries,

04:17.940 --> 04:22.570
I, too, strive to become a bona fide English gentleman, just like him.

04:23.470 --> 04:27.250
The person who holds the key to solving this complex case...

04:27.250 --> 04:28.580
is you!

04:29.230 --> 04:30.490
It's you!

04:31.160 --> 04:32.620
It's you!

04:35.020 --> 04:36.380
It's you!

04:37.430 --> 04:41.150
Th-Then... I have to clean here and over there...

04:41.150 --> 04:43.640
Luke, do you remember this song?

04:56.940 --> 05:11.950
Therefore I'll sing the tale of my dream,

04:59.620 --> 05:03.000
Professor, isn't this Jenis?

05:03.660 --> 05:04.400
Yes.

05:05.550 --> 05:08.150
So, it's already been three years...

05:08.150 --> 05:10.530
since we met her while investigating that curious case.

05:12.330 --> 05:25.600
and how I met you after countless nights.

05:13.670 --> 05:19.920
Now a rising star, Jenis Quatlane was originally one of Professor Layton's disciples.

05:19.920 --> 05:23.860
It all began with a letter she'd sent us.

05:24.740 --> 05:32.490
It was also the first case I worked on with the Professor.

06:11.470 --> 06:25.460
I shall sing to pass my joy to you.

06:25.610 --> 06:39.340
May this happiness last forever.

06:57.530 --> 07:03.560
Luke, that instrument is called Detragan. It was created by Odlaw Whistler.

07:04.650 --> 07:09.570
I heard rumours that it's capable of producing music equivalent to an entire orchestra...

07:10.320 --> 07:12.170
but this astounds me.

07:12.170 --> 07:13.930
It's truly beautiful.

07:13.930 --> 07:16.950
I think Jenis is the true beauty here, Professor.

07:19.590 --> 07:22.200
It's been a while, Professor Layton.

07:23.640 --> 07:28.860
I've been perplexed over a very strange occurrence.

07:30.170 --> 07:35.560
Melina Whistler, a good friend of mine who I lost due to illness a year ago, has come back to life.

07:36.220 --> 07:40.250
Mysteriously, as a seven year old.

07:42.020 --> 07:47.110
She knew every secret I ever had with Melina.

07:47.110 --> 07:49.390
Therefore, I can only believe that she <i>is</i> Melina.

07:50.620 --> 07:52.740
And while I was still in shock, she told me...

07:54.560 --> 07:58.680
Jenis, I have obtained eternal life.

08:08.780 --> 08:13.780
Professor Layton, is there really such a thing as eternal life?

08:14.410 --> 08:16.280
I don't know about that yet, Luke.

08:16.280 --> 08:21.250
But all of our answers may lie in that theatre.

08:21.250 --> 08:22.640
In that case, we should hurry.

08:40.120 --> 08:43.100
Ms. Remi, please drive safely!

08:43.100 --> 08:47.540
If you want to be Professor Layton's assistant, you should at least be used to this by now,

08:48.310 --> 08:49.500
Assistant No. 2.

08:49.500 --> 08:51.350
Assistant No. 2?

08:52.600 --> 08:55.430
I-I'm not an assistant!

08:55.430 --> 08:57.200
Oh dear, am I wrong?

08:57.200 --> 08:59.180
I'm Professor Layton's apprentice!

08:59.930 --> 09:02.170
And not an ordinary apprentice at that!

09:03.410 --> 09:04.770
I'm his number one apprentice!

09:05.760 --> 09:08.580
Did he approve of this?

09:08.580 --> 09:11.320
W-Well... Of course...

09:11.320 --> 09:13.900
Remi, did you take a look at that case?

09:14.810 --> 09:19.910
That girl wasn't the only one who claimed to have obtained eternal life.

09:20.640 --> 09:23.300
After a quick investigation, I found several others.

09:23.300 --> 09:26.430
There's also a rumour spreading across London

09:26.430 --> 09:31.090
that some strange people are selling people eternal life.

09:31.090 --> 09:33.060
Eternal life.

09:34.400 --> 09:36.100
<i>Eternal Kingdom.</i>

09:36.690 --> 09:38.550
"Eternal Kingdom"?

09:39.320 --> 09:42.640
It's the title of the opera Jenis is starring in.

09:43.130 --> 09:47.230
It's a new opera piece Mr. Odlaw Whistler wrote after his long hiatus.

09:47.750 --> 09:50.300
Odlaw Whistler...

09:50.300 --> 09:51.910
Whistler...

09:51.910 --> 09:52.900
I knew it!

09:52.900 --> 09:56.110
The name of Ms. Jenis's friend who came back to life is Melina Whistler.

09:56.840 --> 10:00.070
She has the same surname as Odlaw Whistler, Professor!

10:00.070 --> 10:03.580
That's because Melina is his daughter.

10:05.300 --> 10:09.750
"I dedicate this opera piece to my deceased daughter, Melina."

10:13.110 --> 10:15.530
Professor, there's a picture of him with Professor Schröder!

10:15.530 --> 10:17.750
No need to be surprised, Luke.

10:17.750 --> 10:20.700
This opera piece is based on the Eternal Kingdom,

10:20.700 --> 10:26.150
the legend of Ambrosia, which my mentor, Dr. Schröder, has pursued his entire life.

10:27.420 --> 10:30.770
So far, only a part of its crest has been found.

10:30.770 --> 10:33.700
We don't even know its exact location.

10:33.700 --> 10:35.690
It's a kingdom shrouded by mystery.

10:38.500 --> 10:42.660
The Eternal Kingdom, Ambrosia.

10:43.500 --> 10:45.660
Please continue your investigations, Remi.

10:46.040 --> 10:47.960
Okay, Professor.

10:50.980 --> 10:53.870
This theatre has quite an avant-garde design, don't you think?

10:53.870 --> 10:56.690
Apparently it's an artist's impression of Ambrosia.

10:58.600 --> 11:01.830
What does the legend say, Professor Layton?

11:01.830 --> 11:05.140
Long ago, there was a kingdom that was ruled by a beautiful queen

11:05.140 --> 11:07.850
who loved music from the bottom of her heart.

11:16.730 --> 11:21.110
One day we will meet again.

11:20.130 --> 11:24.670
The queen was loved dearly by her subjects.

11:22.260 --> 11:26.290
So don't be sad anymore.

11:27.770 --> 11:36.960
It's time to set off to a journey in our ephemeral lifetime.

11:40.260 --> 11:44.140
But one day, she fell sick.

11:42.280 --> 11:53.200
Why do you leave us behind?

11:46.350 --> 11:49.640
Her subjects desperately tried to find a cure.

11:50.530 --> 11:53.620
However, the elixir could not be completed in time.

11:53.200 --> 12:03.280
In my lifetime, I will only seek you.

11:58.080 --> 12:00.060
The queen passed away,

12:00.060 --> 12:02.810
and immediately after, the elixir was completed.

12:04.490 --> 12:06.660
The Elixir of Eternal Life.

12:05.390 --> 12:20.670
Therefore I'll sing the tale of my dream,

12:10.120 --> 12:12.970
"If only we'd finished it faster!"

12:12.970 --> 12:16.790
The subjects deeply mourned their queen's death.

12:18.390 --> 12:22.120
Thus, they decided to become immortals

12:21.210 --> 12:32.640
and how I met you after countless nights.

12:22.120 --> 12:25.200
and await the day their queen would open her eyes once more

12:26.390 --> 12:30.130
by using the recently completed elixir.

12:32.630 --> 12:38.020
There's no end

12:33.720 --> 12:37.190
The legend says that Ambrosia still exists somewhere...

12:37.190 --> 12:41.530
waiting for the day their queen is resurrected.

12:38.100 --> 12:46.630
to the tears I'm shedding for you.

12:42.730 --> 12:45.160
But how much of this legend is true remains a mystery.

12:46.940 --> 12:52.130
And so I fall into a deep slumber

12:52.130 --> 13:01.820
as I think of you.

13:10.340 --> 13:10.760
B—

13:11.870 --> 13:13.330
Bravo!

13:26.730 --> 13:30.240
Professor, why aren't they applauding?

13:38.630 --> 13:44.130
I would like to welcome you all to my theatre, the Crown Petone.

13:44.980 --> 13:47.160
You are truly blessed...

13:49.200 --> 13:53.390
for you're all about to witness a miracle.

13:54.850 --> 13:56.390
Bravo!

13:56.390 --> 13:59.860
Finally, the main event is about to start.

13:59.860 --> 14:02.980
I can finally wake up.

14:03.380 --> 14:08.310
As promised on your tickets,

14:08.310 --> 14:15.500
one of you will be granted eternal life.

14:15.500 --> 14:17.610
Eternal life?!

14:17.610 --> 14:21.920
It appears that we're the only ones who didn't know about this, Luke.

14:21.920 --> 14:25.760
However, there is one condition.

14:26.330 --> 14:28.030
A condition?

14:28.030 --> 14:31.580
You're all required to participate in a game.

14:31.580 --> 14:34.970
The winner will be awarded eternal life.

14:35.530 --> 14:37.350
Everyone else, however...

14:38.120 --> 14:42.730
will have to give up their lives.

14:42.730 --> 14:44.110
Our lives?!

14:44.110 --> 14:44.980
What's that supposed to mean?

14:46.030 --> 14:49.080
You're trying to obtain a miracle.

14:49.080 --> 14:52.490
I assume you're all prepared for this.

14:52.880 --> 14:58.080
Eternal life or death? Those are the only options open to us, eh?

14:58.080 --> 14:59.510
Is that really true?

14:59.510 --> 15:00.890
You've got to be kidding me!

15:00.890 --> 15:02.960
I never heard about this!

15:02.960 --> 15:06.770
Fine by me. I only have six months to live anyway.

15:06.770 --> 15:10.080
I'll gladly participate in this game or whatever it is.

15:10.630 --> 15:11.870
I-I'm going home!

15:11.870 --> 15:14.040
Let me go home, now!

15:38.240 --> 15:39.190
Professor!

15:49.030 --> 15:52.550
This is Inspector Groski from Scotland Yard!

15:53.040 --> 15:58.860
I slept in here upon hearing that shady deals would be sealed in the Crown Petone Theatre.

15:58.860 --> 16:01.440
But I never imagined that the trade would concern human lives.

16:01.440 --> 16:04.110
There's no need to worry anymore.

16:04.110 --> 16:11.070
I, Inspector Groski, have just captured the culprit trying to steal your lives!

16:11.070 --> 16:13.960
Are you sure, Inspector Groski?

16:14.900 --> 16:16.280
Oh, if it isn't Layton.

16:16.860 --> 16:18.060
It has been a while.

16:18.060 --> 16:20.140
And you're...

16:20.140 --> 16:21.700
I'm Luke Triton.

16:22.340 --> 16:23.290
Wh-What?!

16:24.620 --> 16:26.590
A p-puppet?!

16:31.520 --> 16:33.170
Professor, the ceiling!

16:43.230 --> 16:44.270
Inspector!

16:59.220 --> 17:00.580
Well then,

17:00.940 --> 17:04.820
let's begin our journey to obtain eternal life.

17:08.750 --> 17:09.110
Luke!

17:09.110 --> 17:10.060
Y-Yes!

17:51.650 --> 17:52.880
The theatre...

17:52.880 --> 17:54.880
It's turned into an enormous ship!

17:55.940 --> 17:57.380
What happened?

17:57.380 --> 17:59.760
We're already far away from land.

17:59.760 --> 18:01.510
Unbelievable!

18:04.170 --> 18:06.520
Wait, what are you doing?

18:06.520 --> 18:09.040
I'm swimming back to land.

18:09.040 --> 18:11.510
I would advise against that idea.

18:21.780 --> 18:26.890
I haven't seen sharks in this sea before.

18:42.150 --> 18:44.350
I can finally go back.

18:52.810 --> 18:55.100
That was a great workout.

18:59.390 --> 19:00.620
So long!

19:19.130 --> 19:22.260
Wait! Don't tell me I'm in the middle of the sea!

19:31.510 --> 19:32.790
Everything is okay.

19:33.990 --> 19:37.290
Leave everything to me.

19:39.100 --> 19:40.360
Let's go...

19:41.840 --> 19:44.660
For your sake as well, since you love music so much.

20:08.060 --> 20:09.680
Don't get in my way!

20:41.700 --> 20:42.590
Cut this out!

20:43.150 --> 20:45.770
This is no time to be playing music!

20:45.770 --> 20:47.600
We've been imprisoned in this theatre...

20:47.600 --> 20:50.720
No, on this ship!

20:55.560 --> 20:57.480
Mr. Whistler,

20:57.480 --> 21:00.650
you're the director of this opera.

21:00.650 --> 21:03.230
Aren't you the one who planned all this?

21:04.010 --> 21:07.850
I only composed this opera because I was asked to.

21:07.850 --> 21:09.100
That is all.

21:11.560 --> 21:12.750
I wonder...

21:13.310 --> 21:15.190
What do we do now, Professor?

21:15.190 --> 21:16.600
Professor Layton!

21:17.910 --> 21:18.890
Jenis.

21:21.750 --> 21:23.670
I'm sorry, Professor Layton.

21:23.670 --> 21:26.040
I dragged you into this.

21:26.040 --> 21:28.430
I didn't expect things to turn out this way.

21:30.000 --> 21:32.160
You don't have to apologise.

21:32.160 --> 21:34.680
It goes without saying that I'd help a lady in distress...

21:37.130 --> 21:38.570
as an English gentleman.

21:41.230 --> 21:43.570
That's something only you would say, Professor Layton.

21:44.190 --> 21:46.780
Where's the girl you mentioned in your letter?

21:46.780 --> 21:50.260
I haven't seen her since this morning.

21:50.260 --> 21:52.000
Even when I asked Mr. Whistler,

21:52.000 --> 21:56.080
he instructed me to concentrate on the play only.

21:56.450 --> 21:58.080
Attention!

21:58.670 --> 22:02.050
How are you enjoying yourselves on the Crown Petone?

22:03.500 --> 22:09.590
Now then, let us begin our game for eternal life with the first puzzle.

22:10.000 --> 22:11.010
Puzzle...

22:11.440 --> 22:12.850
Game?

22:12.850 --> 22:14.020
The rules are simple.

22:14.780 --> 22:19.720
The winner, in other words, the last man standing...

22:19.720 --> 22:22.050
will obtain eternal life.

22:23.900 --> 22:25.250
Professor...

22:25.250 --> 22:29.030
We have no choice but to participate in this situation, Luke.

22:31.420 --> 22:33.040
Let us begin.

22:33.040 --> 22:36.350
Puzzle 001.

22:36.350 --> 22:40.260
Everyone, observe your surroundings very carefully.

22:40.260 --> 22:43.960
Please gather under the oldest thing here.

22:43.960 --> 22:48.010
Now, this puzzle has a time limit.

23:00.680 --> 23:05.790
You have until the music stops.

23:05.790 --> 23:08.070
You don't have time to relax.

23:08.720 --> 23:11.430
"The oldest thing..."

23:11.430 --> 23:13.710
Professor, let's search for it.

23:13.710 --> 23:14.900
Yes.

23:14.900 --> 23:16.130
I'll come with you.

23:16.130 --> 23:17.000
Of course.

23:22.670 --> 23:24.510
Isn't that a fish?

23:24.510 --> 23:25.890
There's no way that's a fish.

23:25.890 --> 23:30.760
Professor, there are many objects that could be the oldest thing here.

23:39.480 --> 23:41.890
I wonder which one is the oldest.

23:43.320 --> 23:46.490
There are many objects that could be the oldest thing here.

23:46.490 --> 23:48.730
Observe your surroundings very carefully.

23:48.730 --> 23:50.360
The oldest thing.

23:52.170 --> 23:53.580
I know the answer, Luke.

23:53.580 --> 23:57.040
Let's go back to the theatre.

24:05.860 --> 24:06.800
Professor?

24:07.540 --> 24:10.970
The clue is hidden in the question of Puzzle 001.

24:14.400 --> 24:16.300
Think about what he said.

24:16.300 --> 24:20.310
The quiz master said to observe our surroundings carefully,

24:20.310 --> 24:23.490
but he never stated that the oldest thing was onboard this ship.

24:23.490 --> 24:26.480
It's true that he didn't say that.

24:26.980 --> 24:31.830
That's because the oldest thing isn't on this ship.

24:31.830 --> 24:33.280
Then where is it?

24:35.250 --> 24:38.030
The oldest thing here, in other words...

24:40.430 --> 24:44.790
are the stars that have existed for several hundred million years.

24:44.790 --> 24:45.980
Oh, right!

24:45.980 --> 24:48.040
I expected nothing less from you, Professor Layton!

24:52.860 --> 24:54.720
Time has run out.

24:55.400 --> 25:01.300
The oldest thing are the stars that keep shining in the night sky.

25:01.300 --> 25:06.020
I'd like to congratulate those who stayed here.

25:06.660 --> 25:10.730
You have received the right to proceed to the next puzzle.

25:31.460 --> 25:34.730
Let us continue to Puzzle 002.

25:36.560 --> 25:42.720
Please gather under the place where you can see the largest crown.

25:42.720 --> 25:45.330
The biggest crown?

25:45.330 --> 25:50.070
Looks like the organiser likes gathering people.

25:50.670 --> 25:55.080
There are several crowns adorning this ship.

25:56.160 --> 26:00.590
It's pretty hard to check their sizes within the time limit.

26:00.590 --> 26:03.590
Which means those crowns are...

26:04.240 --> 26:07.580
...a trap to confuse us.

26:12.070 --> 26:15.950
Professor Layton, can you solve this puzzle?

26:15.950 --> 26:17.730
I wonder...

26:17.730 --> 26:20.960
P-Professor, leave this puzzle to me!

26:20.960 --> 26:22.290
Luke?

26:22.290 --> 26:24.310
Rest assured, Ms. Jenis.

26:24.310 --> 26:27.480
I'm Professor Layton's number one apprentice!

26:29.450 --> 26:31.920
You've found yourself a reliable apprentice.

26:33.640 --> 26:35.490
The largest crown...

26:35.960 --> 26:37.620
Crown...

26:39.270 --> 26:40.410
Crown...

26:41.370 --> 26:44.370
How are you enjoying yourselves on the Crown Petone?

26:44.870 --> 26:46.130
Oh, I've got it!

26:46.130 --> 26:49.040
We're onboard the Crown Petone!

26:50.090 --> 26:52.040
Good thinking, boy!

26:52.040 --> 26:58.530
If I'm not mistaken, the entrance had a big sign saying Crown Petone.

26:58.530 --> 27:00.760
Yes, it was quite big!

27:00.760 --> 27:01.750
Oh, right!

27:01.750 --> 27:03.280
That has to be it!

27:05.270 --> 27:08.280
Let's go, Professor.

27:15.440 --> 27:19.300
Are they already in the lobby now?

27:19.300 --> 27:20.430
Um...

27:20.430 --> 27:23.130
Isn't the lobby in the other direction?

27:24.520 --> 27:27.100
Professor, we're going in the wrong direction.

27:27.100 --> 27:28.160
Let's go back.

27:28.160 --> 27:30.420
Let's keep going, Luke.

27:30.780 --> 27:31.750
But...

27:31.750 --> 27:34.890
The design of the entrance is just another decoy.

27:34.890 --> 27:35.920
What?

27:36.610 --> 27:41.110
The quiz master's intention was to lead everyone to the lobby

27:41.110 --> 27:44.160
and make them think that the crowns were decoys by decorating the whole ship with them.

27:44.160 --> 27:46.350
However, that's not the answer.

27:46.350 --> 27:48.350
Then what is it?

27:48.710 --> 27:51.850
Only one person will obtain eternal life.

27:52.660 --> 27:57.060
The quiz master won't ask questions that can be solved by anyone.

27:57.060 --> 27:59.360
That's the subtlety behind this question.

27:59.800 --> 28:04.530
Remember, we solved Puzzle 001 by gathering in that room.

28:04.530 --> 28:10.920
So he knows we'll try to solve the next question by following the same pattern.

28:10.920 --> 28:14.380
That was a bit too complicated.

28:15.800 --> 28:17.880
Don't worry, you'll see it soon.

28:37.970 --> 28:39.690
Be careful, now.

28:39.690 --> 28:40.650
This is how you do it.

28:42.600 --> 28:44.840
You seem used to this.

28:44.840 --> 28:50.170
I've spent most of my life as the captain of a trading vessel.

28:50.170 --> 28:51.950
The name's O'Donnell.

28:51.950 --> 28:53.710
Now, let's hurry.

29:01.200 --> 29:02.690
Mr. Whistler.

29:02.690 --> 29:03.430
Jenis.

29:10.650 --> 29:12.430
This is my daughter, Melina.

29:14.940 --> 29:17.690
So you've solved the puzzle as well.

29:17.690 --> 29:18.410
Yes.

29:21.190 --> 29:24.700
Man, we've got more rivals now.

29:25.290 --> 29:30.200
B-But you said the right answer was the entrance of the theatre.

29:30.200 --> 29:35.080
I want to obtain eternal life. Of course I lied in order to trick those idiots.

29:36.330 --> 29:41.170
Sheesh, I was worried when you yelled the big hint out loud.

29:41.790 --> 29:42.670
Let me help you.

29:43.540 --> 29:44.520
I did?

29:49.300 --> 29:52.530
Melina, where did you go?

29:52.530 --> 29:53.540
Why?

29:56.900 --> 29:58.860
Why did you call Professor Layton?

29:59.600 --> 30:00.860
Melina...

30:00.860 --> 30:03.230
I cannot let him win!

30:04.670 --> 30:07.110
Aren't you my best friend, Jenis?

30:07.510 --> 30:11.110
Please let our wish come true!

30:12.590 --> 30:14.340
Our wish?

30:27.180 --> 30:30.140
Where on earth are you going with this boat?

30:30.140 --> 30:34.360
Obviously to where you can see the biggest crown.

30:34.360 --> 30:37.900
B-But where is it?

30:37.900 --> 30:41.040
You'll see it in a few minutes, Luke.

30:41.750 --> 30:44.800
You yelled the name of the ship,

30:44.800 --> 30:46.820
yet you still don't know the answer?

30:47.500 --> 30:48.820
May I ask a question?

30:48.820 --> 30:49.490
Miss...

30:50.080 --> 30:51.240
Emily.

30:51.240 --> 30:55.040
Emily, why are you participating in this game?

30:55.850 --> 31:01.540
Yes... the ticket price isn't something a student could afford to buy.

31:02.470 --> 31:05.550
She didn't have to buy a ticket.

31:06.010 --> 31:07.840
She was invited.

31:08.890 --> 31:13.780
I lost my daughter who liked to sing,

31:13.780 --> 31:20.210
so I wanted a young girl to listen to my new opera in place of my daughter.

31:20.210 --> 31:22.610
I sent several invitations.

31:23.120 --> 31:28.610
I decided to send invitations to special girls.

31:29.070 --> 31:32.040
I even consulted the London Times.

31:33.640 --> 31:38.640
Don't let her age fool you. She's the national chess champion.

31:43.150 --> 31:45.390
Just twelve participants left, eh?

31:46.490 --> 31:53.170
But with only them remaining, it doesn't seem it'll be too hard to obtain eternal life.

31:53.170 --> 31:55.470
Where does your confidence come from?

31:55.470 --> 31:59.330
I shall obtain eternal life.

31:59.330 --> 32:02.500
And then my beauty will illuminate forever.

32:07.480 --> 32:12.490
This reminds me of my novel <i>Murder on the River Thames</i>.

32:12.490 --> 32:17.740
The characters were riding a nine year-

32:15.650 --> 32:17.740
Are you Annie Doretchi?

32:18.170 --> 32:19.730
The mystery writer?

32:19.730 --> 32:21.050
Oh, my.

32:21.050 --> 32:27.320
If it isn't the recently widowed, rich, beautiful flower of high society...

32:27.320 --> 32:30.710
I'm honoured that you know me, Ms. Leidori.

32:30.710 --> 32:33.220
You know, I like reading gossip magazines.

32:33.220 --> 32:39.630
It's a huge honour to be so close to such a famous personality as yourself.

32:39.630 --> 32:40.890
Speaking of which,

32:40.890 --> 32:47.260
this bearded gentleman is Mr. Bargland, the CEO of the World Fleet conglomerate.

32:49.900 --> 32:52.820
And this gentleman was known as the "legendary left foot,"

32:52.820 --> 32:55.420
a former national football player for England.

32:55.420 --> 32:57.370
You're Pierre Starbuck?

32:57.370 --> 33:01.180
I knew you looked familiar.

33:01.180 --> 33:03.160
"Legendary left foot," huh?

33:03.160 --> 33:04.210
It's nothing but a bad joke now.

33:07.680 --> 33:12.530
I need eternal life in order to get my old leg back.

33:20.540 --> 33:22.410
She's a bit shy.

33:24.090 --> 33:25.400
Mr. Whistler,

33:25.400 --> 33:30.400
is the story about Melina reincarnating and obtaining eternal life true?

33:31.730 --> 33:33.610
Who told you that?

33:35.680 --> 33:37.040
Of course it's not true.

33:37.040 --> 33:40.930
I only named her after my deceased daughter.

33:40.930 --> 33:42.120
That's all.

33:42.120 --> 33:42.480
But...

33:42.480 --> 33:43.710
Jenis,

33:44.400 --> 33:48.900
I really appreciate the fact that you want Melina to return,

33:48.900 --> 33:51.730
but she's no longer alive.

33:52.800 --> 33:57.200
That's why I wanted to live her share too.

33:58.150 --> 33:59.610
It was around that time

33:59.610 --> 34:04.420
when that man requested I create an opera with the theme of eternal life.

34:04.420 --> 34:06.290
So, I accepted the commission.

34:07.810 --> 34:10.570
I never thought it would end up like this.

34:18.160 --> 34:19.740
Professor, the ship!

34:29.590 --> 34:31.820
It looks like a crown!

34:33.840 --> 34:37.790
Congratulations to those on the boats.

34:38.500 --> 34:42.180
You're one step closer to eternal life.

34:46.770 --> 34:47.990
Professor!

34:49.300 --> 34:53.360
Please enjoy your ride through the sea.

35:17.990 --> 35:20.430
That was another good workout.

35:26.610 --> 35:28.180
H-Hey!

35:30.420 --> 35:34.100
My Scotland Yard spirit cannot be defeated!

35:34.100 --> 35:36.930
Justice will always prevail!

36:12.230 --> 36:14.090
Professor Layton!

36:14.090 --> 36:14.480
Jenis!

36:47.150 --> 36:50.270
What did the police say about your daughter's disappearance?

36:50.670 --> 36:53.230
They still don't have any clues...

36:54.050 --> 36:56.830
Nina has an excellent memory...

36:57.890 --> 37:00.780
She wouldn't get lost.

37:00.780 --> 37:04.370
Can I see a picture of her?

37:04.370 --> 37:05.160
Of course.

37:31.970 --> 37:33.760
Sis...

37:57.530 --> 38:01.500
Professor Schröder, is this from Ambrosia?

38:01.500 --> 38:07.850
It's the only artifact of Ambrosia, and it's very important.

38:08.520 --> 38:17.180
Eternal life is the ultimate desire that has fascinated humans since ancient times.

38:17.180 --> 38:20.020
Death is inevitable for all of us.

38:20.570 --> 38:23.110
It's only natural.

38:23.110 --> 38:31.010
That's why people can't help but try and escape their fate.

38:31.010 --> 38:36.120
Professor, does Ambrosia still exist?

38:37.180 --> 38:39.480
At least I hope so.

38:39.480 --> 38:45.620
Ambrosia's historical significance is unfathomable, regardless of its legend of immortality.

38:46.180 --> 38:50.060
I'd love to explore it myself.

38:50.060 --> 38:51.640
News flash.

38:52.040 --> 38:53.150
Early this morning,

38:53.150 --> 38:58.390
rescue boats carrying a large number of people have drifted ashore causing a huge stir.

38:58.940 --> 39:01.990
It appears that they were part of the audience at the Crown Petone Theatre

39:01.990 --> 39:05.280
which had suddenly vanished last night.

39:05.280 --> 39:09.660
However, several people, including singer Jenis Quatlane and composer Odlaw Whistler

39:09.660 --> 39:11.940
are still missing.

39:11.940 --> 39:13.230
I repeat.

39:15.170 --> 39:16.360
I've got to go.

39:16.360 --> 39:17.660
Ah, Remi!

39:21.680 --> 39:23.670
Oh boy...

39:29.630 --> 39:31.540
What is this island?

39:31.540 --> 39:34.390
At a glance, it looks like an uninhabited island.

39:34.390 --> 39:35.380
Don't tell me...

39:38.770 --> 39:40.820
I see...

39:40.820 --> 39:41.560
I knew it!

39:43.670 --> 39:45.110
There's no doubt!

39:45.110 --> 39:47.530
This is Ambrosia!

39:48.310 --> 39:49.650
Ambrosia?

39:49.650 --> 39:53.850
To be precise, where Ambrosia once was.

39:55.690 --> 39:59.660
This is my - amateur historian Marco Brock's - scrapbook.

39:59.660 --> 40:04.670
It contains various bits of information about the Eternal Kingdom Ambrosia

40:04.670 --> 40:06.760
that I've compiled in my spare time.

40:06.760 --> 40:08.460
It seems well used.

40:08.460 --> 40:10.130
A history nerd?

40:10.980 --> 40:12.590
Look at this!

40:12.590 --> 40:15.760
It has the same insignia!

40:15.760 --> 40:18.850
Indeed, it does look like Ambrosia's crest.

40:18.850 --> 40:21.770
I've seen this in Professor Schröder's office before.

40:21.770 --> 40:26.520
Finally! My greatest desire has come true!

40:26.520 --> 40:29.780
I never believed that I'd see this in my life.

40:29.780 --> 40:31.350
I see!

40:31.350 --> 40:33.360
So this is Ambrosia!

40:33.360 --> 40:34.280
Which means...

40:34.280 --> 40:38.700
The elixir for eternal life is here somewhere?

40:39.640 --> 40:40.960
Hey, look at that!

40:47.270 --> 40:49.040
Food, eh?

40:49.040 --> 40:50.590
Wine?

40:52.460 --> 40:55.110
For the participants.

40:55.110 --> 40:56.540
I've been waiting for this.

40:56.540 --> 40:59.100
I'm so thirsty.

40:59.100 --> 41:02.100
Maybe this is our reward for solving the puzzle?

41:02.100 --> 41:05.600
What if this <i>is</i> the new puzzle?

41:14.550 --> 41:15.920
I don't care.

41:15.920 --> 41:17.210
I'm eating!

41:22.800 --> 41:24.630
It's delicious.

41:24.630 --> 41:26.460
You're right.

41:37.470 --> 41:40.420
This is all like a dream.

41:40.420 --> 41:43.290
This game, this island...

41:43.290 --> 41:46.600
eating together with you...

41:47.130 --> 41:49.230
and gazing at the sea...

41:49.820 --> 41:54.740
Isn't it normal to eat outside if you're on an archaeological dig?

41:56.210 --> 41:58.240
The beach isn't the same as an excavation area.

41:58.240 --> 42:03.250
Though you might find the latter more romantic.

42:14.120 --> 42:14.840
Here.

42:21.910 --> 42:24.520
You can hear the sound of the waves when you do this.

42:26.010 --> 42:29.210
Though I can never really hear it myself.

42:30.420 --> 42:31.360
I can hear it.

42:31.360 --> 42:32.780
What? Really?

42:45.120 --> 42:46.930
That's a beautiful song.

42:48.820 --> 42:50.500
The ocean taught me that.

42:50.500 --> 42:51.560
The ocean?

43:00.510 --> 43:01.790
Did you hear th—

43:01.790 --> 43:03.130
H-Huh?

43:05.590 --> 43:06.930
Mr. Whistler!

43:06.930 --> 43:08.430
Melina's gone!

43:08.430 --> 43:10.570
She was right there just a moment ago.

43:10.570 --> 43:13.250
She probably wandered off to play somewhere else.

43:13.250 --> 43:15.780
She'll be back. You don't have to worry.

43:16.570 --> 43:17.990
Your attention please.

43:18.380 --> 43:21.900
Your break is about to end.

43:30.700 --> 43:31.790
Professor!

43:31.790 --> 43:33.560
There are wolves!

43:34.750 --> 43:39.510
Should we make you their dessert?

43:39.910 --> 43:41.090
Run for it!

43:51.190 --> 43:53.070
That's strange!

43:53.070 --> 43:56.050
They don't understand what I'm saying.

43:56.050 --> 43:58.070
They're most likely being controlled by someone.

44:02.950 --> 44:04.550
Jenis, Luke! Run!

44:04.550 --> 44:05.710
Yes!

44:10.120 --> 44:13.130
That's it! I can't run anymore!

44:13.130 --> 44:17.090
You're already running, you stubborn granny.

44:17.570 --> 44:18.640
There's a river!

44:39.540 --> 44:40.530
Is that...

44:42.890 --> 44:44.110
A castle?

44:48.400 --> 44:51.400
Puzzle 003.

44:51.400 --> 44:57.130
Please cross the river and approach the black castle that you see in front of you.

44:57.130 --> 44:58.710
Professor!

44:58.710 --> 45:00.650
We need to do something about those wolves first.

45:05.350 --> 45:06.890
Damn persistent wolves!

45:07.380 --> 45:09.430
Professor, look at that!

45:13.850 --> 45:14.840
Cages?

45:21.550 --> 45:22.860
I've got it, Luke.

45:22.860 --> 45:26.200
We'll get through this with the help of those cages.

45:35.040 --> 45:37.430
Come and get us, you damn wolves!

45:40.170 --> 45:41.130
Let's go!

45:41.860 --> 45:42.170
Wait!

45:43.270 --> 45:44.300
Over here!

45:44.300 --> 45:46.250
Now! Let go!

45:50.640 --> 45:51.830
Crap!

45:59.860 --> 46:02.840
Haven't we just imprisoned ourselves instead?

46:02.840 --> 46:05.010
It's the answer to the puzzle.

46:05.010 --> 46:06.240
I see.

46:06.240 --> 46:12.580
One, two... One, two... One, two...

46:13.130 --> 46:14.540
How silly.

46:14.540 --> 46:21.140
It's far more effective to get inside the cage ourselves than to try and capture all the wolves.

46:21.140 --> 46:24.750
You have to think outside the box in order to solve this puzzle, Luke.

46:24.750 --> 46:26.380
I expected nothing less of you, Professor.

46:26.820 --> 46:29.170
Stupid or not, we have to do it.

46:31.730 --> 46:33.700
Okay, step-by-step.

46:33.700 --> 46:38.690
One, two... One, two... One, two...

46:41.270 --> 46:44.700
That university professor, Layton,

46:44.700 --> 46:49.280
might be pretty sharp despite his appearance.

46:55.740 --> 46:57.940
We're in trouble again, Professor!

46:59.110 --> 47:00.710
Let's go!

47:00.710 --> 47:02.500
Not again, Professor?!

47:04.310 --> 47:06.920
There's nothing wrong with sweating a little, Luke.

47:06.920 --> 47:09.010
Even an English gentleman needs to sweat now and then.

47:27.300 --> 47:28.130
Get into that hut!

47:28.130 --> 47:28.900
Right!

47:33.780 --> 47:34.390
Hurry up!

47:34.390 --> 47:35.200
Jenis!

47:35.200 --> 47:36.370
Okay!

47:50.030 --> 47:51.570
Interesting.

47:51.570 --> 47:54.760
It looks like somebody lives on this island.

48:05.240 --> 48:07.400
Professor, what are you doing?

48:07.400 --> 48:10.780
I've figured a way out of our dilemma.

48:10.780 --> 48:12.760
I'll help you.

48:25.380 --> 48:26.170
It's done.

48:28.530 --> 48:29.670
Let's go!

48:29.670 --> 48:30.440
But...

48:30.440 --> 48:31.380
Hold tight!

48:38.560 --> 48:41.820
Professor Layton, is this a mini helicopter?

48:41.820 --> 48:43.400
That was the idea, but...

48:51.480 --> 48:52.780
Go faster, Professor!

48:53.250 --> 48:54.920
The wolves are gaining on us!

48:54.920 --> 48:57.600
I can't accelerate, Luke.

49:05.460 --> 49:09.360
It feels like we're jumping around like a grasshopper.

49:09.360 --> 49:11.430
Are you all right, Jenis?

49:11.430 --> 49:14.030
If you're scared, you can hold onto me.

49:14.030 --> 49:16.870
No, I'm not scared.

49:16.870 --> 49:18.480
I'm really enjoying this!

49:21.200 --> 49:22.990
You are?!

49:22.990 --> 49:23.600
Yes!

49:32.030 --> 49:34.500
I can see the black castle, Professor.

49:34.500 --> 49:35.490
We're going to land.

49:35.490 --> 49:37.040
Hold on tight! Anywhere you can!

49:37.040 --> 49:37.660
Okay!

49:38.510 --> 49:39.660
I have to pilot...

50:14.300 --> 50:16.650
Welcome to my castle, Hershel.

50:20.040 --> 50:23.620
Please gather on the terrace of the castle.

50:31.150 --> 50:32.590
I'm amazed you made it.

50:32.590 --> 50:35.580
I thought you'd become their lunch.

50:37.410 --> 50:38.150
Luke.

50:40.490 --> 50:44.600
Let's move onto Puzzle 004.

50:45.330 --> 50:50.860
You should see four doors that lead to four different towers.

50:50.860 --> 50:53.260
Please look at the floor.

50:54.860 --> 50:58.350
Professor, there are letters on the floor.

50:58.350 --> 51:00.620
One of the four towers

51:00.620 --> 51:04.130
was once the king's bedroom.

51:05.030 --> 51:09.860
Now, find the correct door leading to the king's bedroom

51:09.860 --> 51:13.130
and enter that tower.

51:16.130 --> 51:19.440
H, I, K and G...

51:19.440 --> 51:21.110
What are they supposed to represent?

51:27.470 --> 51:30.670
I'm so close to eternal life!

51:32.400 --> 51:35.290
Tower, king, tower...

51:37.050 --> 51:38.950
I've figured it out, Professor!

51:38.950 --> 51:40.670
This time I must be right!

51:41.510 --> 51:45.680
King is spelled as "K-I-N-G."

51:45.680 --> 51:50.430
If you add "IN" between "K" and "G" it becomes "KING!"

51:50.430 --> 51:55.390
In other words, the correct door stands between "K and "G."

51:56.760 --> 51:58.450
Great job, Luke.

52:01.500 --> 52:05.700
Luke, will you be okay by yourself?

52:05.700 --> 52:08.830
There's something I need to investigate.

52:08.830 --> 52:09.940
But...

52:09.940 --> 52:14.940
The goal of this game might not be eternal life.

52:35.930 --> 52:37.480
Don't worry.

52:37.480 --> 52:41.310
You'll be fine as long as you choose the same answers as the professor.

52:56.480 --> 52:58.080
Wait!

52:58.080 --> 52:58.820
This is...

53:06.860 --> 53:08.680
I'll definitely save you!

53:08.680 --> 53:10.040
Hold on!

53:33.350 --> 53:35.030
The Professor has...

53:35.030 --> 53:36.210
Don't worry!

53:36.210 --> 53:38.170
He'll definitely come back!

53:43.490 --> 53:44.240
Why?!

53:44.240 --> 53:47.450
Th-This should be the right room!

54:07.090 --> 54:08.530
You are...

54:08.530 --> 54:10.670
Luke, do you know him?

54:10.670 --> 54:12.810
Who are you?

54:12.810 --> 54:14.930
My name is de Scole.

54:14.930 --> 54:19.240
You could say that I'm a first-class scientist who's passionate about pushing the boundaries of science.

54:20.240 --> 54:21.630
Well then...

54:23.170 --> 54:27.490
This game will now terminate here as per the organiser's wish.

54:28.810 --> 54:30.860
But didn't you say that the last participant remaining in the game

54:30.860 --> 54:33.860
wins the prize of eternal life?

54:33.860 --> 54:36.050
What are you planning, de Scole?!

54:36.050 --> 54:37.850
Bring Emily over.

54:40.920 --> 54:42.710
Wh-What are you doing?

54:44.920 --> 54:46.850
What are you going to do to her?

54:46.850 --> 54:48.570
Don't come any closer!

54:48.570 --> 54:49.180
Leave her alone!

54:51.770 --> 54:52.680
Jenis!

55:02.020 --> 55:05.040
Hey!

55:06.320 --> 55:08.780
I'm here!

55:12.910 --> 55:19.890
So, when that ship exploded, what I saw were lifeboats?

55:19.890 --> 55:23.950
Yes, they were filled with the audience from the Crown Petone Theatre.

55:23.950 --> 55:25.820
It caused a huge uproar in London!

55:25.820 --> 55:28.000
That's strange.

55:28.000 --> 55:31.550
I saw two kinds of lifeboats.

55:31.550 --> 55:32.300
Two?

55:32.300 --> 55:33.650
Yeah.

55:33.650 --> 55:36.490
They went in opposite directions.

55:37.140 --> 55:38.520
I've got a bad feeling about this.

56:10.150 --> 56:12.850
As I expected, she lived here.

56:16.650 --> 56:18.000
A Song of the Sea?

56:35.270 --> 56:37.460
What beautiful scenery.

56:45.170 --> 56:48.150
I'm here to see you, Melina.

56:51.520 --> 56:52.590
Don't...

56:52.990 --> 56:54.870
Don't get in my way!

57:25.180 --> 57:28.300
How long have you been living here?

57:29.310 --> 57:31.710
Since I became ill.

57:33.370 --> 57:37.720
Papa said the climate of this island would be good for me, so he brought me here.

57:38.370 --> 57:41.240
It's why my condition's improved so much.

57:41.240 --> 57:42.280
Improved?

57:43.280 --> 57:45.020
Instead of merely recovering from your illness,

57:45.020 --> 57:48.730
I heard that you were reincarnated and received eternal life.

57:49.170 --> 57:51.600
Yes, that's right.

57:51.600 --> 57:53.910
I died because of my illness.

57:54.740 --> 57:56.170
I didn't die!

57:56.170 --> 57:57.280
I'm fine!

57:57.280 --> 57:58.920
I was brought here!

57:58.920 --> 57:59.970
No, I can't tell him!

57:59.970 --> 58:01.080
He's going to notice!

58:01.460 --> 58:02.180
But...

58:04.000 --> 58:04.970
Melina.

58:04.970 --> 58:06.080
I'm not Melina!

58:06.080 --> 58:07.550
Yes, I'm Melina.

58:07.550 --> 58:09.540
No! I want to go home!

58:09.540 --> 58:11.540
Stop! Stop!

58:17.930 --> 58:19.010
You are...

58:38.660 --> 58:39.410
This way!

58:39.410 --> 58:40.280
Okay!

58:53.940 --> 58:56.750
Okay! Okay! I get it!

58:56.750 --> 58:59.550
Hey! Don't touch my notebook!

59:17.050 --> 59:19.290
I'll protect you, Jenis!

59:19.290 --> 59:19.860
Luke!

59:34.090 --> 59:35.260
Ms. Remi!

59:35.900 --> 59:38.930
Sorry for the wait, Assistant No. 2.

59:38.930 --> 59:40.110
I'm his number one apprentice!

59:40.110 --> 59:42.240
I'm Grosky from Scotland Yard.

59:42.240 --> 59:44.820
You're under arrest!

01:00:14.860 --> 01:00:16.670
Are you all right?

01:00:16.670 --> 01:00:18.760
Professor Layton!

01:00:19.880 --> 01:00:20.740
Nina.

01:00:22.120 --> 01:00:23.080
Nina?

01:00:27.600 --> 01:00:29.820
I've seen your Papa and Mama.

01:00:29.820 --> 01:00:31.770
You've seen them?

01:00:31.770 --> 01:00:34.510
They're worried sick about you.

01:00:34.510 --> 01:00:37.070
Professor, de Scole's taken Emily!

01:00:37.070 --> 01:00:38.070
Yes.

01:00:38.070 --> 01:00:39.880
Come, let's hurry.

01:00:45.340 --> 01:00:46.100
Yes.

01:00:49.810 --> 01:00:52.480
The time has finally come.

01:00:53.050 --> 01:00:57.490
I told you everything would be fine as long as you listened to me.

01:00:57.490 --> 01:01:02.800
You're the one who caused this entire mess by handing tickets out unnecessarily!

01:01:04.040 --> 01:01:05.550
Everything was within my calculations.

01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:23.860
Mr. Whistler, you're the organiser?

01:01:24.530 --> 01:01:27.370
Please come this way, Emily.

01:01:27.990 --> 01:01:30.010
Where's everyone else?

01:01:30.010 --> 01:01:31.470
Why am I the only one here?

01:01:31.470 --> 01:01:35.760
Because I'm gifting you eternal life, of course.

01:01:36.600 --> 01:01:38.160
This is...

01:01:38.160 --> 01:01:38.960
The Detragan.

01:01:40.240 --> 01:01:42.360
I brought it with me from the ship.

01:01:42.360 --> 01:01:46.260
The Detragan is a unique musical instrument,

01:01:46.640 --> 01:01:50.770
yet at the same time, it's the machine that will gift you eternal life.

01:01:51.250 --> 01:01:54.090
I'm its creator.

01:02:10.980 --> 01:02:12.670
Now, hurry! This way!

01:02:12.670 --> 01:02:14.040
What? Um...

01:02:14.040 --> 01:02:15.610
W-Wait!

01:02:15.610 --> 01:02:19.420
I want to gift my grandfather with eternal life!

01:02:19.420 --> 01:02:22.820
The doctors say that he has less than a month left to live.

01:02:22.820 --> 01:02:25.580
Isn't it supposed to be some kind of medicine?

01:02:26.570 --> 01:02:28.840
Then I must bring my Grandfather here.

01:02:30.600 --> 01:02:32.680
I'm afraid I can't allow that.

01:02:34.170 --> 01:02:35.490
Stop it!

01:02:35.490 --> 01:02:37.810
Let go of me!

01:02:37.810 --> 01:02:39.270
Let go!

01:02:45.490 --> 01:02:46.580
Don't!

01:02:49.250 --> 01:02:50.490
Stop it!

01:02:51.540 --> 01:02:52.620
Stop it!

01:02:56.770 --> 01:02:57.520
Are you okay?

01:02:57.520 --> 01:02:58.270
Yes.

01:03:01.910 --> 01:03:07.930
Mr. Whistler, you never had any intention of gifting the winner with eternal life, did you?

01:03:09.430 --> 01:03:13.750
What you're doing is not gifting people with eternal life,

01:03:13.750 --> 01:03:15.430
but taking lives away!

01:03:16.130 --> 01:03:18.190
What makes you think that?

01:03:18.590 --> 01:03:21.890
I've finally solved the mystery.

01:03:21.890 --> 01:03:25.440
The scary truth behind this game, that is.

01:03:25.440 --> 01:03:27.320
What do you mean, Professor?

01:03:28.900 --> 01:03:33.630
A year ago, despite Melina's impending death...

01:03:33.630 --> 01:03:36.500
No, precisely because her death was drawing near,

01:03:36.500 --> 01:03:41.350
you threw a large sum of money into creating a giant musical instrument, the Detragan.

01:03:42.130 --> 01:03:46.420
De Scole was the one who helped you create it.

01:03:47.590 --> 01:03:50.720
The Detragan is not a normal instrument.

01:03:50.720 --> 01:03:55.600
It's also an atrocious machine that can temporarily record people's memories

01:03:56.170 --> 01:03:57.850
and transfer them into other people's brains.

01:03:57.850 --> 01:03:59.100
What?

01:03:59.100 --> 01:04:01.440
Transfer memories?!

01:04:02.840 --> 01:04:06.070
However, the machine's transfer function is not perfect.

01:04:07.350 --> 01:04:09.180
Even though the machine can transfer memories,

01:04:09.180 --> 01:04:14.580
as time passes, the original memories override the transferred memories.

01:04:15.740 --> 01:04:17.850
Nina told me...

01:04:19.030 --> 01:04:24.090
Can you tell me whom you were with again?

01:04:24.720 --> 01:04:25.510
Yes!

01:04:25.510 --> 01:04:27.210
I was with Melina!

01:04:37.980 --> 01:04:40.230
After several failures,

01:04:40.230 --> 01:04:46.570
you frantically started searching for a brain that would accept Milena's memories.

01:04:50.170 --> 01:04:55.410
That's why you sent invitations to successful young girls like her.

01:04:56.900 --> 01:05:02.650
I assume de Scole came up with the idea of the game and offering eternal life as the prize.

01:05:03.360 --> 01:05:09.710
And that's because you wanted to transfer Melina's memories as soon as possible, Mr. Whistler.

01:05:10.430 --> 01:05:12.510
I expected nothing less from you, Layton.

01:05:12.510 --> 01:05:14.820
When did you notice that I was involved?

01:05:15.510 --> 01:05:17.650
I had a suspicion,

01:05:17.650 --> 01:05:21.680
but it was confirmed once I saw this tasteless, black castle.

01:05:22.050 --> 01:05:26.310
I rapidly realised that the one pulling the strings was you, de Scole!

01:05:28.320 --> 01:05:31.740
A boy is fine too!

01:05:32.150 --> 01:05:34.740
I have to preserve Melina's memories!

01:05:32.670 --> 01:05:34.740
No! Stop it!

01:05:34.740 --> 01:05:37.330
There's no time left!

01:05:37.330 --> 01:05:37.990
Stop it!

01:05:47.680 --> 01:05:48.500
What?

01:05:51.790 --> 01:05:53.260
Wh-Who took it?!

01:05:53.260 --> 01:05:54.590
Who?!

01:05:54.590 --> 01:05:55.510
Layton!

01:05:55.510 --> 01:05:56.710
Did you take it?

01:05:56.710 --> 01:05:58.100
We've got to hurry!

01:05:58.460 --> 01:06:02.700
There's a time limit on how long the Detragan can store memories.

01:06:02.700 --> 01:06:05.170
Calm down, Mr. Whistler.

01:06:05.170 --> 01:06:07.480
I know who stopped the machine.

01:06:09.510 --> 01:06:17.910
And they hold the key to explaining all these events.

01:06:17.910 --> 01:06:20.220
It's something that exceeds even your wildest dreams.

01:06:20.220 --> 01:06:21.740
Who is it?

01:06:21.740 --> 01:06:24.130
You still haven't noticed?

01:06:24.130 --> 01:06:26.500
Someone who hides their true identity

01:06:26.500 --> 01:06:29.500
and wants you to stop committing this grave mistake more than anyone else.

01:06:31.400 --> 01:06:33.510
Who wants to stop this more than anyone else?

01:06:34.170 --> 01:06:35.700
That person is you!

01:06:39.930 --> 01:06:41.960
M-Ms. Jenis?!

01:06:44.630 --> 01:06:48.640
It's time to reveal everything, Jenis.

01:06:49.190 --> 01:06:50.460
No...

01:06:50.460 --> 01:06:51.270
I should really say Melina.

01:06:55.110 --> 01:06:57.530
Yes, I'm Melina!

01:06:58.780 --> 01:07:04.290
I was terrified when I first discovered that I was in my friend Jenis's body.

01:07:07.720 --> 01:07:13.300
I couldn't believe that she accepted my existence.

01:07:21.640 --> 01:07:25.840
She drew her consciousness into the dormant parts of her brain

01:07:25.840 --> 01:07:27.970
and allowed me to do as I pleased.

01:07:31.800 --> 01:07:38.090
It was a wonderful feeling being able to walk around freely since I'd been bedridden since my illness.

01:07:39.320 --> 01:07:42.320
O-Oh my god!

01:07:43.520 --> 01:07:45.210
Congratulations, Whistler.

01:07:45.210 --> 01:07:48.080
We have realised our goal.

01:07:48.730 --> 01:07:53.710
But one day, I started feeling that this was all wrong.

01:07:54.340 --> 01:07:58.220
I can't continue living by sacrificing my friend.

01:07:58.830 --> 01:08:01.850
You were the one who sent me that letter, weren't you?

01:08:01.850 --> 01:08:05.470
I was correct in placing my trust in you, Professor Layton.

01:08:06.180 --> 01:08:08.940
I felt that you could stop Papa.

01:08:08.940 --> 01:08:11.330
I deeply prayed that you would stop him.

01:08:11.330 --> 01:08:14.730
I can't remain in her body forever.

01:08:15.140 --> 01:08:19.860
But as long as this machine exists, Papa will continue to transfer my memories.

01:08:21.540 --> 01:08:23.180
Melina...

01:08:25.610 --> 01:08:30.270
I've heard so much about you from Jenis.

01:08:30.270 --> 01:08:33.740
She's always admired you.

01:08:44.470 --> 01:08:45.940
Melina...

01:08:45.940 --> 01:08:49.830
I did all of this for you.

01:08:49.830 --> 01:08:51.760
Thank you, Papa.

01:08:51.760 --> 01:08:53.770
I really appreciate that.

01:08:53.770 --> 01:08:58.770
But I'm begging you, please put an end to this lunacy.

01:08:58.770 --> 01:09:03.430
I can't bear the thought of living without you,

01:09:03.430 --> 01:09:03.990
that's why...

01:09:06.700 --> 01:09:08.620
Professor, the ceiling!

01:09:12.620 --> 01:09:14.090
What on earth?

01:09:19.990 --> 01:09:21.510
Melina!

01:09:34.900 --> 01:09:36.810
What are you planning, de Scole?!

01:09:37.420 --> 01:09:42.740
I require Melina's memories to accomplish my true goal.

01:09:42.740 --> 01:09:44.650
Your true goal?

01:09:45.270 --> 01:09:48.160
The resurrection of Ambrosia.

01:09:53.990 --> 01:09:58.880
It all started when I found the complete crest of Ambrosia.

01:10:02.810 --> 01:10:08.340
Layton, do you know what the lines stand for?

01:10:13.320 --> 01:10:14.180
A musical score?

01:10:14.180 --> 01:10:16.000
Exactly.

01:10:16.000 --> 01:10:17.920
The music of Ambrosia.

01:10:17.920 --> 01:10:23.060
In other words, the first key to opening the gates of Ambrosia is hidden within the crest.

01:10:23.060 --> 01:10:25.700
"A Song of the Stars" is carved on the emblem.

01:10:26.250 --> 01:10:27.450
"A Song of the Stars"?

01:10:27.970 --> 01:10:30.850
And you're the second key, Melina.

01:10:30.850 --> 01:10:33.290
I'm the second key?

01:10:33.920 --> 01:10:38.700
I failed to revive Ambrosia with the first key alone.

01:10:38.700 --> 01:10:45.720
Later on, I realised that the crest is pointing to a second key.

01:10:46.710 --> 01:10:47.720
"A Song of the Sea."

01:10:48.170 --> 01:10:49.610
The stars and the sea.

01:10:49.610 --> 01:10:54.480
When the two melodies intertwine, the legendary kingdom of Ambrosia will revive.

01:10:55.120 --> 01:10:59.980
I never realised that you were after the kingdom itself!

01:10:59.980 --> 01:11:02.540
By the time I solved the riddle of the crest,

01:11:02.540 --> 01:11:08.180
the only person who could correctly recall "A Song of the Sea"

01:11:08.180 --> 01:11:09.990
could no longer sing.

01:11:11.230 --> 01:11:13.490
However, her memories were preserved.

01:11:14.120 --> 01:11:18.670
I longed for a perfect copy of her memories more than Whistler!

01:11:36.060 --> 01:11:37.350
Ms. Jenis!

01:11:42.720 --> 01:11:44.050
Luke!

01:11:47.930 --> 01:11:49.070
Be careful, everyone!

01:11:49.760 --> 01:11:50.740
Stop them!

01:11:50.740 --> 01:11:54.410
You'll have to sing if you want to save them, Melina!

01:12:02.160 --> 01:12:05.510
Come, this is the final stage I've prepared for you.

01:12:09.170 --> 01:12:10.840
Sing, Melina!

01:12:10.840 --> 01:12:11.800
"A Song of the Sea!"

01:12:18.890 --> 01:12:20.640
Now, sing, Melina!

01:12:50.190 --> 01:12:51.570
Are you all right, Luke?

01:12:52.840 --> 01:12:55.850
Ambrosia will rise!

01:13:03.500 --> 01:13:04.440
Run for the exit!

01:13:06.690 --> 01:13:07.560
Luke!

01:13:07.560 --> 01:13:08.440
Okay!

01:13:11.050 --> 01:13:11.700
Professor!

01:13:18.120 --> 01:13:18.950
Luke!

01:13:34.320 --> 01:13:35.300
Why?

01:13:36.030 --> 01:13:37.910
Why won't it revive?!

01:13:37.910 --> 01:13:39.930
Do it again! Sing!

01:13:39.930 --> 01:13:40.950
Sing!

01:13:53.860 --> 01:13:54.870
I get it!

01:14:02.630 --> 01:14:04.080
Why...

01:14:04.080 --> 01:14:05.740
Why?

01:14:05.740 --> 01:14:07.000
Why?!

01:14:14.190 --> 01:14:17.130
If the gates to the kingdom refuse to open,

01:14:17.130 --> 01:14:20.910
then I'll open them myself!

01:15:05.450 --> 01:15:06.570
What's that?

01:15:06.570 --> 01:15:08.360
It's enormous!

01:15:08.360 --> 01:15:10.940
Professor, what's de Scole up to?

01:15:30.610 --> 01:15:31.290
Go!

01:15:32.140 --> 01:15:33.660
Detra Gigant!

01:15:58.610 --> 01:15:59.710
Melina!

01:16:00.340 --> 01:16:01.380
Melina!

01:16:01.380 --> 01:16:02.560
Mr. Whistler!

01:16:03.550 --> 01:16:06.300
Please... save Melina!

01:16:07.850 --> 01:16:10.080
If we use the power of the kingdom...

01:16:10.080 --> 01:16:11.950
Remi, take care of the others.

01:16:11.950 --> 01:16:13.160
Let's go, Luke!

01:16:13.160 --> 01:16:14.590
Understood!

01:16:21.050 --> 01:16:22.710
Reveal yourself, Ambrosia!

01:16:23.430 --> 01:16:27.560
Give me your treasured wisdom!

01:16:34.460 --> 01:16:35.690
Come!

01:16:36.140 --> 01:16:37.940
Reveal yourself!

01:16:45.550 --> 01:16:46.370
That's...

01:16:48.500 --> 01:16:49.410
Layton?

01:16:51.620 --> 01:16:53.110
Interesting...

01:16:53.110 --> 01:16:55.330
You dare to challenge me with that piece of junk?

01:16:59.970 --> 01:17:01.220
Ms. Melina!

01:17:03.150 --> 01:17:06.420
I'll get rid of anyone who stands in my way!

01:17:15.920 --> 01:17:16.480
Stop it!

01:17:18.860 --> 01:17:19.780
Get lost!

01:17:30.150 --> 01:17:32.710
Professor, I'll save her!

01:17:32.710 --> 01:17:34.250
No! It's too dangerous!

01:17:38.230 --> 01:17:39.190
I'll be fine!

01:17:39.190 --> 01:17:40.710
Please have faith in me, Professor!

01:17:41.400 --> 01:17:42.470
I'm...

01:17:43.360 --> 01:17:45.220
going to be a true English gentleman!

01:17:48.540 --> 01:17:50.130
All right, go and save her, Luke!

01:17:50.130 --> 01:17:50.810
Yes!

01:18:14.710 --> 01:18:16.390
This is the best I can do.

01:18:16.390 --> 01:18:18.090
That's more than enough!

01:18:39.300 --> 01:18:41.260
Please hang in there, Ms. Melina!

01:18:41.260 --> 01:18:42.930
I'm coming to save you!

01:19:09.410 --> 01:19:10.850
Luke Triton...

01:19:10.850 --> 01:19:13.040
What a cheeky brat!

01:19:15.710 --> 01:19:17.480
Ms. Melina!

01:19:28.030 --> 01:19:29.820
Luke!

01:19:42.540 --> 01:19:43.800
Professor!

01:19:50.200 --> 01:19:51.850
Blown to smithereens, huh?

01:20:03.530 --> 01:20:05.270
What a lucky man!

01:20:40.360 --> 01:20:42.190
Thank you, Luke.

01:20:43.130 --> 01:20:46.060
That was nothing.

01:21:01.560 --> 01:21:02.510
You still have a long way to go.

01:21:03.220 --> 01:21:05.750
I wonder about that, de Scole.

01:21:06.800 --> 01:21:08.860
The puzzle of the crest.

01:21:08.860 --> 01:21:09.880
What?

01:21:09.880 --> 01:21:13.090
You only solved half the puzzle.

01:21:13.590 --> 01:21:15.190
Only half?

01:21:15.190 --> 01:21:18.350
"A Song of the Stars" and "A Song of the Sea" aren't enough.

01:21:19.070 --> 01:21:22.810
You need three keys to revive Ambrosia, de Scole!

01:21:24.300 --> 01:21:29.690
Your mistake was to see the crest from only one perspective!

01:21:31.440 --> 01:21:36.070
You can see a third melody when you rotate the crest...

01:21:38.620 --> 01:21:39.830
The sun!

01:21:39.830 --> 01:21:42.600
That's right, the third key is

01:21:45.000 --> 01:21:46.460
"A Song of the Sun"!

01:21:47.710 --> 01:21:49.860
As long as the sun doesn't sing,

01:21:49.860 --> 01:21:53.970
the dawn will never rise for Ambrosia, de Scole!

01:22:01.760 --> 01:22:04.450
Melina, sing "A Song of the Sea"!

01:22:04.450 --> 01:22:05.180
Okay!

01:23:59.050 --> 01:24:01.300
Layton!

01:24:09.990 --> 01:24:13.750
I will investigate Ambrosia with my own hands!

01:24:38.190 --> 01:24:39.430
De Scole!

01:24:45.600 --> 01:24:46.850
Professor!

01:24:49.600 --> 01:24:50.160
Professor!

01:24:50.840 --> 01:24:54.810
At this rate, it'll only be a matter of seconds before that monstrous thing explodes!

01:24:54.810 --> 01:24:56.820
Hurry up! Run away!

01:25:00.150 --> 01:25:01.820
What are we going to do, Professor?

01:25:07.520 --> 01:25:09.190
Let's go, Luke.

01:25:09.710 --> 01:25:10.560
Melina.

01:25:33.070 --> 01:25:34.100
Melina!

01:25:35.140 --> 01:25:36.030
Are you okay?

01:25:36.030 --> 01:25:36.980
Melina!

01:25:38.880 --> 01:25:40.030
Melina...

01:25:41.090 --> 01:25:42.230
Melina!

01:26:41.010 --> 01:26:42.350
Melina...

01:26:44.620 --> 01:26:45.790
Melina.

01:26:50.160 --> 01:26:51.850
M-Melina.

01:26:54.320 --> 01:26:55.170
Papa...

01:26:55.170 --> 01:26:56.470
Melina!

01:26:56.470 --> 01:26:58.480
Thank heavens you're all right.

01:26:58.480 --> 01:27:02.040
Don't go off and leave me behind, all right?

01:27:02.040 --> 01:27:03.480
I promised you, didn't I?

01:27:02.170 --> 01:27:03.480
Papa.

01:27:03.480 --> 01:27:05.070
I'll protect you.

01:27:05.070 --> 01:27:10.070
Come, let's go home and play the piano again, and sing—

01:27:08.520 --> 01:27:10.070
I'm sorry.

01:27:10.550 --> 01:27:16.620
I've caused you grief and suffering both before and after my death.

01:27:16.620 --> 01:27:17.210
That's not true!

01:27:17.210 --> 01:27:19.150
It's my fault!

01:27:19.150 --> 01:27:20.950
I couldn't save you...

01:27:20.950 --> 01:27:22.710
That was my fate!

01:27:22.710 --> 01:27:24.160
It's not your fault, Papa.

01:27:24.160 --> 01:27:25.550
But I need—

01:27:25.550 --> 01:27:29.300
I want you to open a new page in your life!

01:27:30.870 --> 01:27:33.190
I don't want to!

01:27:39.240 --> 01:27:41.310
I know...

01:27:42.570 --> 01:27:48.460
that even if I disappear, my feelings for you will remain.

01:27:51.600 --> 01:27:54.890
Thus, I'll always be with you.

01:27:54.890 --> 01:27:56.780
It's just that you won't be able to see me.

01:27:56.780 --> 01:27:59.080
Don't say any more! Please!

01:27:59.650 --> 01:28:01.620
Melina...

01:28:01.620 --> 01:28:06.590
Papa, before your performances, don't you always say

01:28:06.590 --> 01:28:07.800
"No matter how—"

01:28:07.800 --> 01:28:14.600
"No matter how far apart we are, I remember you and feel close to you whenever I play the piano."

01:28:14.600 --> 01:28:18.370
And that's because I'm always thinking of you.

01:28:19.310 --> 01:28:20.600
That's why we're always together.

01:28:21.580 --> 01:28:25.070
Whenever you think of me,

01:28:25.070 --> 01:28:27.570
I'll be by your side.

01:28:34.480 --> 01:28:36.410
Farewell, Papa.

01:28:38.090 --> 01:28:41.420
Thank you for loving me.

01:28:43.270 --> 01:28:45.360
Now, it's time for me to go.

01:28:58.530 --> 01:28:59.680
Jenis,

01:28:59.680 --> 01:29:02.740
I'm sorry I borrowed your body for so long.

01:29:02.740 --> 01:29:04.590
Don't go, Melina.

01:29:04.590 --> 01:29:06.440
If you go, I will—

01:29:06.440 --> 01:29:08.330
It'll be okay, Jenis.

01:29:08.330 --> 01:29:10.490
I have to go.

01:29:10.490 --> 01:29:11.950
For Papa's sake as well.

01:29:12.670 --> 01:29:17.700
I'm so glad that you cherish the pendant I gave you.

01:29:18.900 --> 01:29:20.200
Melina!

01:29:21.040 --> 01:29:22.730
Farewell.

01:29:22.730 --> 01:29:27.450
From now on, live your life as Jenis Quatlane.

01:29:32.020 --> 01:29:33.340
Goodbye, Papa.

01:29:33.760 --> 01:29:34.990
Melina!

01:29:35.900 --> 01:29:37.340
Goodbye, Luke,

01:29:38.000 --> 01:29:39.950
the future gentleman.

01:29:41.930 --> 01:29:43.350
Ms. Melina...

01:29:44.170 --> 01:29:47.440
And goodbye to you too, Professor Layton.

01:29:48.920 --> 01:29:49.940
Melina...

01:29:50.790 --> 01:29:55.390
I'm really glad that you're my last memory.

01:30:07.800 --> 01:30:09.090
Melina?

01:30:09.090 --> 01:30:10.200
Meli—

01:30:24.590 --> 01:30:28.610
Professor Layton, she left.

01:30:29.230 --> 01:30:31.710
Even though she was standing by my side just a moment ago...

01:30:38.320 --> 01:30:39.410
Ms. Jenis!

01:30:39.410 --> 01:30:42.120
Ms. Melina didn't leave!

01:30:42.620 --> 01:30:44.230
Luke.

01:30:44.230 --> 01:30:47.880
Even if she's no longer here, didn't she say

01:30:47.880 --> 01:30:50.050
that her memories will always be here?

01:30:50.870 --> 01:30:53.130
Isn't that right, Professor Layton?

01:30:54.570 --> 01:30:55.880
Yes.

01:30:57.060 --> 01:31:00.390
Her memories are always by my side.

01:31:00.970 --> 01:31:03.800
Her memories are always by my side...

01:31:08.430 --> 01:31:11.880
Mr. Whistler, you are under arrest.

01:31:32.180 --> 01:31:35.630
Please! Let me play one last piece!

01:32:17.610 --> 01:32:22.780
Ambrosia's immortality had nothing to do with eternal life.

01:32:22.780 --> 01:32:29.020
It was about grieving the queen's death and sleeping forever.

01:32:29.800 --> 01:32:33.530
You have solved yet another puzzle, Professor.

01:32:59.130 --> 01:33:07.580
Professor, I wonder if Ms. Melina was the reincarnation of Ambrosia's queen?

01:33:07.580 --> 01:33:10.380
Maybe, Luke.

01:33:10.380 --> 01:33:16.150
The kingdom awoke from its long slumber and has now begun a new era.

01:33:16.850 --> 01:33:18.080
Their queen...

01:33:18.080 --> 01:33:21.980
the Eternal Diva's soul has returned.
